Fix the RFCOMM length indicator in bthost's data frames.

TS 07.10 section 5.2.1.4 defines the length indicator as the length of
the information field.  bthost_send_rfcomm_data() writes sizeof(struct
rfcomm_hdr) + len into it, which is three too many: the address, the
control and the length octet are the header, not information.

The frame is otherwise right -- uih_len is len + 4 and the FCS lands
in the last octet -- so only the field is wrong, and only on data
frames that bthost sends.  A receiver that believes the field reads
past the end of the frame.  Wireshark's RFCOMM dissector calls such a
frame malformed, and the Inferno stack this image exists to test
refuses it, which is how it was found.

This is emulator/bthost.c, BlueZ's host stack for its own tests, and
not the RFCOMM in the Linux kernel.

--- a/emulator/bthost.c
+++ b/emulator/bthost.c
@@ -3723,10 +3723,10 @@
 	hdr = (struct rfcomm_hdr *) uih_frame;
 	hdr->address = RFCOMM_ADDR(1, channel * 2);
 	hdr->control = RFCOMM_CTRL(RFCOMM_UIH, 0);
 	if (len > 127) {
-		hdr->length  = RFCOMM_LEN16(cpu_to_le16(sizeof(*hdr) + len));
+		hdr->length  = RFCOMM_LEN16(cpu_to_le16(len));
 		memcpy(uih_frame + sizeof(*hdr) + 1, data, len);
 	} else {
-		hdr->length  = RFCOMM_LEN8(sizeof(*hdr) + len);
+		hdr->length  = RFCOMM_LEN8(len);
 		memcpy(uih_frame + sizeof(*hdr), data, len);
 	}
